# Copy to frontend/.env.local for local development. Never commit the real one.
#
# There is only one variable here, and that is not an oversight.
#
# Vite bakes every VITE_* value into the JavaScript bundle at build time, and the bundle
# is downloaded by anyone who opens the site. So nothing secret can ever live in a
# frontend environment file — no API keys, no database URLs, no tokens. Those all belong
# to the backend (see backend/.env.example), which keeps them on the server.
#
# Everything else the app needs it asks the API for at runtime: who is signed in, what
# they may do, which institutes exist. That is why this file stays one line long.

# Where the browser sends API calls.
#
#   local development   http://localhost:8000/api/v1   (the API on its own port)
#   production          /api/v1                        (relative — see below)
#
# In production this is RELATIVE on purpose. The Docker image builds the frontend into
# the API container, so the pages and the API answer on the same host. A relative path
# means the same image works on a *.run.app URL, on the custom domain, and locally,
# with no rebuild between them — and because it is the same origin, the auth cookies
# always travel and there is no CORS to configure.
#
# The Dockerfile sets VITE_API_URL=/api/v1 itself, so you do not set it for a deploy.
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8000/api/v1
